0006-drop-nonpositive-melts-endmember.patch
==========================================

Numerical guard in VapoRock src/vaporock/equil.py, System._calc_liquid_chempot
(upstream https://gitlab.com/ENKI-portal/vaporock.git @ 0159678). The edited
region is unchanged by 0001 and 0002, so this applies on the pin and on a
tree that already carries those two. Everything above the first `diff --git`
line is a note; git apply ignores it.

Classification: reference bugfix, same class as 0002. Mainline already claims
to evaluate liquid chemical potentials for a stated oxide composition. This
patch adds no species and raises no temperature band.

Defect
------
LiquidMelts activity (the mask in LiquidMelts.m that adds R*T*log(x[i]) when
x[i] != 0) drops an exact-zero endmember mole, setting mu to 0 and skipping
the log, but it logs a negative mole. The MELTS liquid endmembers are not
the oxides. Ca, Mg and Na are carried as CaSiO3, Mg2SiO4 and Na2SiO3, and
leftover Si is the SiO2 endmember. A Ca-rich, Si-poor CMAS melt (Mathieu
2009 admitted row 1: SiO2 49.1, Al2O3 0.05, CaO 41.29, MgO 5.06, Na2O 4.5
wt% at 1673.15 K) projects to a negative SiO2 endmember mole. That log is
NaN, and the NaN spreads through every present-oxide gas row.
eval_gas_abundances then returns 0 finite rows out of 38 (22 NaN, 16 -inf).

Absent Fe, Ti, K and Cr are not the cause. An in-simplex Fe-free melt stays
finite, and those absent oxides are the existing -inf inventory mask. This
is not a transcribed constant, so there is no literature page citation.

Fix
---
Before chem_potential, if any endmember mole is negative, copy the vector
and set those moles to 0, the same boundary LiquidMelts already uses for
exact zero. When every mole is non-negative the original array is passed
through, so an in-simplex composition stays bit-identical. The stored
mol_liq is left unchanged. NaN values are not replaced after the call, and
the simulator adapter is not changed.

Gate boxes that already carry 0001 and 0002 pick this up by applying this
file with git apply, then running patches/scripts/enginepatch.sh verify
vaporock. STATUS marks this patch applied, so verify includes its changed
lines and still skips 0003, 0004 and 0005.

diff --git a/src/vaporock/equil.py b/src/vaporock/equil.py
index 779a737..b12ae59 100644
--- a/src/vaporock/equil.py
+++ b/src/vaporock/equil.py
@@ -345,17 +345,29 @@ class System:
         OXIDES = self.OXIDES
 
 
         model_oxides = melt_comps.index[1:]
         ind_oxides = [np.where(OXIDES==iox)[0][0] for iox in model_oxides]
 
         mu0_endmem = np.array([liq_phs.gibbs_energy(T, P, mol=imol)
                                for imol in np.eye(mol_liq.size)])
-        mu_endmem = liq_phs.chem_potential(T, P, mol=mol_liq).squeeze()
+        # A non-positive MELTS endmember mole is outside the simplex.
+        # LiquidMelts activity logs x[i] whenever x[i] != 0, so a
+        # negative dependent mole (SiO2, when the silicate endmembers
+        # oversubscribe Si) is NaN, and that NaN spreads through every
+        # oxide chemical potential. Exact zero already drops out inside
+        # LiquidMelts (mu set to 0, no log). Feed that same boundary
+        # into the call. Positive moles are passed through unchanged,
+        # so an in-simplex composition is bit-identical.
+        mol_for_mu = mol_liq
+        if np.any(np.asarray(mol_liq) < 0.0):
+            mol_for_mu = np.asarray(mol_liq, dtype=float).copy()
+            mol_for_mu[mol_for_mu < 0.0] = 0.0
+        mu_endmem = liq_phs.chem_potential(T, P, mol=mol_for_mu).squeeze()
 
         mu0_oxides = np.dot(mol_liq_oxides, mu0_endmem)[ind_oxides]
         mu_oxides = np.dot(mol_liq_oxides, mu_endmem)[ind_oxides]
 
         return mu_oxides, mu0_oxides
 
     def _calc_gas_species_abundances(self, T, logfO2, mu_oxides, mu0_oxides,
                                      G_gas_species, method='activity'):
